A||N||11||2xsess||A/C Bummer||||9||
Z||000000||2xsess||09-19-2000||10:44 PM||||I had a local shop check out my A/C system. They disconnected the plug on the accumulator and jumped it, then with the car running and the A/C on, the compressor didn't kick on. They said that means I need a new compressor. Is that right? The guy also said I should get a new accumulator because when the compressor goes bad it might blow metal bits through the system. <BR>The shop wants $525 (parts and labor) to fix it. <BR>   How hard is it to change the compressor? Can I do it myself and just have them do the oiling and charging? Would the system have to be purged before the new parts are installed? I just bought the car and there are so many other things I need/want to spend the money on. <P><BR>Thanks<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||09-19-2000||10:53 PM||||$525? That's cheap for the amount of work and parts involved.<P>If the compressor didn't try to spin over one of 4 things is going on. <P>They didn't jumper the right thing or enough things. I'm guessing they where intent on jumpering the low pressure switch. The one on the ACC/dryer is the LP switch as I recall so that rules this out. (Unless the jumper wasn't making good contact.)<P>There is a control problem in the dash. <P>There is a problem with the wires to AC clutch or the AC clutch magnet is bad. This can be replaced w/o opening the sytem at all.<P>The high pressure switch is bad/open. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||09-19-2000||11:00 PM||||He used a light tester and checked the jump to make sure it was good. I don't really know too much about A/C. But I love working on my Fiero, learning more about it. I guess I should check out the wires because I already found the speedometer wires cut and the O2 sensor wire spliced. <P>Basically Ogre, what I'm asking is....should I avoid trying this?<P>Thanks alot,<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||capt_ron||09-20-2000||01:33 AM||||Jeff,<BR>Does this system have any pressure on it? The shop should have hooked up the gauges to it to check. You can check the compressor by trying to turn the end outside the pulley, if it turns with a little resistance then you may not have to replace it. If you can't turn it at all then a rebuilt compressor will cost you about $200. If the system is under pressure then you can have it evacuated by a local shop. I replaced the compressor on my v-6 in about an hour. I also had to replace the dryer/accummilator and orfice tube. The orfice tube has a screen and will trap some of the trash from the compressor but you will still have to flush the lines, condensor, and evaporator.<BR>Use new O-rings to put everything back together and either have the shop pull the system down and recharge it or do it yourself. You will need a 609 certification to purchase the R-12, a set of gauges, and a vacumm pump to do it yourself. The 609 is easy to get <A HREF="http://www.epatest.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.epatest.com</A>  and R-12 is around $20-25/can. You will need 3 cans if I remember, maybe 4. The vacumm pump can come from a window a/c or old refridgerator. Just install a fitting on the suction side to hook the gauges to. Of course by the time you do all this the shops price doesn't look so bad.<BR>My car has an intermittant problem energizing the compressor that I found is the relay. I'll replace it one of these days.<BR>Good Luck<P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||09-20-2000||07:22 AM||||The system has 0 pressure. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||09-20-2000||08:41 AM||||I paid over $800 to repair my R12 system in my 87 a couple of years ago.  It still works great.<P>Twin Lakes converted my 88 to r134a with used compressor for less than $400.  I don't remember the exact cost because other repairs were done at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||09-20-2000||08:42 AM||||Did the shop check the relay?  The one that controls the compressor is located on the rear firewall (behind the seat) left of center.  If it is bad it will not energize the clutch.<P>I have converted 5 of my cars to R134 without any problems for the last 3 years.  R-134 does not require a certification to buy or use and costs around $4 to $5 per can.  Some of the new Interdynamics kits do not require opening the system.  You charge with an R134/oil charge in addition to the R134.  Of course if your system is under pressure you will have to have it evacuated by a certified person and the old R-12 recycled.<P>But since yours has no pressure this should not be an issue.<P>Getting the compressor is not a terrible job although it can seem so to someone not accustomed to working underneath a Fiero.<P>The compressor is held on by three bolts, two on the top bracket and one on the bottom that  is also used to tighten the belt.<P>Disconnect the negative battery wire to avoid unwelcomed sparks when wrenching near the starter motor.  (Believe me that you can suffer serious injury by getting a wrench into this wire).  <P>Jack the car way up (or use ramps) so you can get under it and have elbow room to pull on wrenches.  Put  it on jack stands (never work under a car supported only by a jack).<P>Remove the heat shield under the belt tensioning bolt, it is held on by two 10mm self tapping screws.  This gives a better access to the bottom bolt.<P>Disconnect the high pressure switch wire on the rear head of the compressor.  Also remove the hose assembly from the compressor.<P>Now the fun begins.<P>If you lay just right you'll see the two bolts attaching the compressor to the top bracket.  The one on the driver's side is a bolt and nut combination.  This one is a bear to get to but it is possible using a ratchet with extensions and a combination wrench.  The other side is a bit easier to get to.  It threads into the compressor flange from the inside of the bracket.  You can get to it from the bottom or from the top (I think from the bottom is easier).  Removing the battery and its heat shield makes it easier to get to from the top.<P>If you can't get to the driver side bolt you can remove the three bolts attaching the A/C bracket to the head from the top.  <P><BR>I'd suggest replacing the dryer and orifice tube when replacing the compressor just to be on the safe side.<P>A new compressor (not rebuilt) will cost anywhere from $300 to $500.  Rebuilt units are around $200.  Most shops warned me against using a rebuilt comressor with a R134 conversion since the head pressures will be a bit higher.<P>Good luck.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||capt_ron||09-20-2000||01:31 PM||||Jeff,<BR>I forgot to put this in my last post but the people on the A/C forum can give you a lot of advice. Pay particular attention to the advice about not converting to R-134a, especially with the InterDynamics kit. <A HREF="http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/</A> <BR> <P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000008||2xsess||09-20-2000||06:54 PM||||Actually I wasn't even considering converting to 134A because it takes sooooooo long to cool down. My 97 Transport has 134A and I don't really care for it.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-20-2000||08:17 PM||||The slight reduction in cooling capacity usually attributed to R134 conversions isn't much of an issue in Fiero. The AC system in these cars is already far higher capacity than it needs be to cool such a small space. Fiero has an AC system that's easily as much capacity as cars with three times the volume of space to cool.<P>The primary problem with interdynamics kit is it's marketing. The print material with the kit makes it look like R134 is a simple drop in. It's not. Leaving the old mineral in the system is just one issue.<P>Converting to R134 properly is about the same as replacing the compressor. I.E. replace the ACC/Dryer, replace the orifice tube. dump the mineral oil out of the compressor and replace it with PAG or Ester.<P>There are 2 reasons to replace the ACC/dryer. First is that it has a designed life of about 7 years. second is the new one will have desicant that is R134 or R12 compatible. Desicant removes the water and prevents acids that form in the system from causing harm.<P>There's a bunch of notes about this in my cave in the MVAC2 atricle.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2xsess||09-22-2000||08:15 AM||||When I check out the relay, should I just check for voltage? How do I check it out?<P>I've been thinking it over and I'm going to go with 134A for the simple reason that if it gets low I can recharge it myself.<P>Thanks for all the help!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||5
Z||000011||Carrolles||09-22-2000||08:45 AM||||I've converted 4 of my cars to R-134 and don't notice any reduction in cooling.  This includes an 89 Bonneville (3 years without problems so far), 85SE (1 year with no problems so far), 86 Calais (2 years no problems) and 88 Camry (just did it last month).<P>||||reg||1
